==Post-match==
[[Ramón Díaz]] was interviewed shortly after the defeat. When asked about his team's performance, the River Plate manager stated: "Our opponent is very prominent; they're a very tough team to play. Their pace is very rare to see in Argentine football". He then added: "We didn't play well at all in the first half, but we improved in the second and at our best moment they scored. There's nothing to reproach." Regarding their upcoming fixtures, Díaz commented: "The squad was left disappointed after being unable to show the level of football they've been displaying in the league. They'll channel that frustration into the [<nowiki/>[[1996–97 Argentine Primera División#Torneo Apertura|Torneo Apertura]]], which I'm sure we'll win."<ref>{{cite magazine |title=¡Qué desilusión! |magazine=El Gráfico |date=November 1996 |language=es}}</ref>

Juventus reached the [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|final]] of the [[1996–97 UEFA Champions League]], which they lost to [[Borussia Dortmund]] 3–1.<ref>{{Cite web |title=European Competitions 1996-97 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec199697.html |access-date=2026-02-10 |website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |last=Ross |first=James M. |archive-date=21 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230221054818/https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec199697.html |url-status=live }}</ref> They would win the [[1996–97 Serie A]], however, which qualified them to the [[1997–98 UEFA Champions League|1997–98 edition]] of the tournament.<ref name=":2" /> River Plate followed a similar suit, as they were also unable to retain their continental title.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Copa Libertadores 1997|url=https://www.rsssf.org/sacups/copa97.html|access-date=2026-02-10|website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation|last=Ciullini|first=Pablo|last2=Stokkermans|first2=Karel|archive-date=16 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230416140746/http://www.rsssf.org/sacups/copa97.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Nevertheless, they were crowned champions of the 1996 Torneo Apertura, the first of the three consecutive league titles they would go on to win.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Final Tables Argentina 1991-2000|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesa/arghist-pro1990s.html|access-date=2026-02-10|website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation|last=Gorgazzi|first=Osvaldo José|last2=Kurhy|first2=Víctor Hugo|archive-date=16 December 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071216024747/http://www.rsssf.com/tablesa/arghist-pro1990s.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

